🌳 LeetCode 111 — Minimum Depth of Binary Tree
1. Problem Statement

Given the root of a binary tree, find the minimum depth of the tree.

The minimum depth is the number of nodes along the shortest path from the root node to the nearest leaf node.

A leaf node is a node with:

left == null
right == null
Example
        3
       / \
      9   20
         /  \
        15   7

Paths to leaves:

3 → 9          → 2 nodes
3 → 20 → 15    → 3 nodes
3 → 20 → 7     → 3 nodes

Therefore:

Answer = 2

2. Logic :

The problem asks for the shortest path from the root to a leaf. I use recursive DFS. For every node, I calculate the minimum depth of its left and right subtrees and add one for the current node.

The important case is when one child is null. I cannot treat a null child as depth zero because that would represent a path that doesn't exist. So I treat a missing child as Integer.MAX_VALUE, effectively infinity, so it will never be selected as the minimum.

If the current node is a leaf, I return one because the current node itself contributes one to the depth

3. Code :

class Solution {
    public int minDepth(TreeNode root) {
        if(root==null){
            return 0;
        }
        if(root.left==null && root.right==null){
            return 1;
        }
        return 1+Math.min(root.left!=null?minDepth(root.left):Integer.MAX_VALUE,root.right!=null?minDepth(root.right):Integer.MAX_VALUE);
    }
}
